urce>J. Biol. Chem.</source><volume>272</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>31877</fpage><lpage>31884</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9395535</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Tauraetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Taura</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Krebber</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Silver</surname><given-names>P.A.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A member of the Ran-binding protein family, Yrb2p, is involved in nuclear protein export</article-title><source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source><volume>95</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>7427</fpage><lpage>7432</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9636166</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Welchetal1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Welch</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Franke</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kohler</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Macara</surname><given-names>I.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>RanBP3 contains an unusual nuclear localization signal that is imported preferentially by importin-&#x003b1;3</article-title><source>Mol. Cell. Biol</source><volume>19</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>8400</fpage><lpage>8411</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10567565</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wenetal1995"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wen</surname><given-names>W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Meinkoth</surname><given-names>J.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsien</surname><given-names>R.Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Taylor</surname><given-names>S.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Identification of a signal for rapid export of proteins from the nucleus</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>82</volume><year>1995</year><fpage>463</fpage><lpage>473</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7634336</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> BHK21, baby hamster kidney&#x0003b; bPKI, biotinylated PKI&#x0003b; GFP, green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; GGRanBP1, GFP&#x02013;GFP&#x02013;RanBP1 fusion protein&#x0003b; GST, glutathione <italic>S</italic>-transferase&#x0003b; HA<sub>3</sub>-RanBP3, triple hemagglutinin&#x02013;tagged RanBP3&#x0003b; LMB, leptomycin B&#x0003b; NES, nuclear export signal&#x0003b; NLS, nuclear localization signal&#x0003b; NPC, nuclear pore complex&#x0003b; PKI, protein kinase A inhibitor&#x0003b; RanBP3, Ran-binding protein 3&#x0003b; RanGAP, Ran GTPase&#x02013;activating protein&#x0003b; RBD, Ran-binding domain&#x0003b; STV, streptavidin.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Crm1 binds directly and specifically to RanBP3. (A) Crm1 binds directly to RanBP3 in the presence of RanQ69L and NES peptide. Recombinant Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> (200 nM) was incubated with GST-tagged RanBP3 attached to glutathione&#x02013;Sepharose beads in the presence of RanQ69L (2 &#x003bc;M), MVM NES peptide (2 &#x003bc;M), or LMB (2 &#x003bc;M), as shown, and bound protein was analyzed by immunoblotting. (B) Yeast dihybrid conjugation assays. <italic>S</italic>. <italic>cerevisiae</italic> HF7c (MAT-a) that express bait (GAL4DBD) fusions of importin-11, Cas, importin&#x003b2;, and Crm1 were mated to <italic>S</italic>. <italic>cerevisiae</italic> W303 (MAT-&#x003b1;) that express the VP16 activation domain alone or as prey fusions with Ran or RanBP3. Growth of diploids is shown after replica plating to L<sup>&#x02212;</sup>H<sup>&#x02212;</sup>W<sup>&#x02212;</sup> plates, which support growth only of those yeast in which the bait fusion interacts with the prey fusion protein.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>RanBP3 stimulates Crm1-mediated nuclear protein export. (A) Permeabilized HeLa cells were preloaded with bPKI/FITC&#x02013;STV&#x02013;NLS export cargo and then exposed to Crm1 (90 nM), Ran (1.0 &#x003bc;M), &#x000b1;RanBP3 (1.0 &#x003bc;M), &#x000b1;LMB (200 nM), as shown. Export activity was monitored as a loss of nuclear fluorescence over 8 min at 30&#x000b0;C. (B) Quantitation of PKI export. Nuclear fluorescence was measured for &#x0223c;50 randomly chosen cells in each sample and expressed as the mean &#x000b1; 1 SD. Results are representative of two independent experiments. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>RanBP3 increases the affinity of Crm1 for Ran:GTP. (A) Various combinations of Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> (330 nM), GST-RanBP3 (250 nM), and RanQ69L (2.0 &#x003bc;M) were incubated with MVM NS2 NES peptide&#x02013;conjugated beads (5 &#x003bc;l). Bound and unbound fractions were analyzed by anti-His<sub>6</sub> (Crm1), anti-Ran (RanQ69L), and anti-GST (GST-RanBP3) immunoblotting. The detected species are indicated at right. (B) Wild-type Ran was loaded with &#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP and incubated with immobilized Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> (16 pmol) on Ni<sup>2+</sup>-NTA beads in the absence or presence of GST-RanBP3 (120 nM) or MVM-NS2 NES peptide (0, 100, or 4,000 nM). After washing, bound Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP was counted and graphed as percent maximal binding. (C) Assay was repeated as in B, with either GST-RanBP3 or GST-NF mutant added at indicated molar ratios. Values reported as average of three samples &#x000b1; SD.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>RanBP3 increases the affinity of Crm1 for Ran:GTP and NES. (A) RanBP3 is a weak coactivator of RanGAP. GST-RanGAP (0.15 nM) was added to preassembled complexes of Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP (40 nM) +RanBP3 (1 or 10 &#x003bc;M) for 4 min at 30&#x000b0;C. After filter binding to nitrocellulose, protein-associated Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP was detected by scintillation counting. (B) RanBP3 increases Crm1-mediated protection of Ran:GTP from RanGAP. GST-RanGAP (30 nM) was added to preassembled complexes of Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP (40 nM), cleaved RanBP3 (1 &#x003bc;M), Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> (330 nM), and NES peptide (200 nM). (C) RanBP3 increases the Crm1 affinity for NES. Titration of Crm1 and Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP with an increasing concentration of NES peptide (0&#x02013;20 &#x003bc;M) was performed in the presence (&#x02022;) or absence (&#x025aa;) of RanBP3 (1 &#x003bc;M).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>The FxFG-containing domain and RBD of RanBP3 are required for full export activity. (A) Diagram of deletion mutants, showing domain structure of RanBP3. Mutants are illustrated without the 27-kD amino terminal GST tag. Locations of the NLS, two FxFG repeats, and the RBD are indicated. (B) Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> was exposed to glutathione&#x02013;Sepharose beads coated with various GST-tagged RanBP3 fragments. Unbound protein was identified by Coomassie brilliant blue (CBB) staining. Bound protein was identified by both Coomassie staining and immunoblotting with an anti-His<sub>6</sub> antibody. (C) Deletion mapping of Crm1-dependent export activity. The PKI export assay was performed as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Fig. 1</xref>, using 100 &#x003bc;g/ml of each GST-RanBP3 fragment. Nuclear fluorescence was quantitated in &#x0223c;50 randomly chosen cells for each experimental condition and is presented as the mean &#x000b1; SD. Export activity is represented as loss of fluorescence. (D) The RanGAP protection assay was repeated as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Fig. 4</xref> with equimolar levels of GST-RanBP3 deletion mutants.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>RanBP3 exports from the nucleus in a Crm1-dependent manner. (A) Triple HA-tagged RanBP3 (HA<sub>3</sub>-RanBP3) was transiently expressed in BHK21 cells, which were trypsinized and replated with an equal number of GSN2 cells and then fused by addition of polyethyleneglycol. Cells were fixed and stained with anti-HA Texas red&#x02013;conjugated secondary antibodies. Nuclei were identified by DAPI staining. (B) BHK21 cells were cotransfected with vectors encoding HA<sub>3</sub>-RanBP3 and GGRanBP1 before cell&#x02013;cell fusion. Cells were incubated with LMB (200 nM) after fusion, or before and after fusion, or with no LMB. After 1 h, cells were prepared as described above. Bars, 5 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>The Crm1&#x02013;RanBP3&#x02013;NES&#x02013;Ran:GTP complex is dissembled by RanBP1 and RanGAP. (A) GST-RanGAP (30 nM) or GST-RanGAP plus RanBP1 (1.0 &#x003bc;M) were added to complexes of Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP (40 nM), GST-RanBP3 (1.0 &#x003bc;M), Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> (330 nM), and MVM-NS2 NES peptide (200 nM). After 4 min at 34&#x000b0;C, samples were bound to nitrocellulose filters and washed. Filter-bound Ran&#x003b3;[<sup>32</sup>P]GTP was detected by scintillation counting. (B) Glutathione&#x02013;Sepharose beads loaded with GST-RanBP3, Crm1-H<sub>6</sub>, RanQ69L, and MVM-NS2 NES peptide, were aliquoted and resuspended in either buffer or buffer containing RanBP1 (1.0 &#x003bc;M). Samples of bound and unbound proteins were analyzed by immunoblotting.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>The Crm1&#x02013;RanBP3&#x02013;NES&#x02013;Ran:GTP complex interacts with the NPC. (A) Crm1 immunoprecipitations were performed in the presence or absence of excess RanBP3. 4 &#x000d7; 10<sup>8</sup> digitonin-permeabilized HeLa cells were solubilized, after which the indicated transport factors were added, incubated for 1 h at 4&#x000b0;C, and precipitated with anti-Crm antibody and protein A&#x02013;Sepharose for 1 h. After washing 4&#x000d7; in solubilization buffer, bead associated proteins were immunoblotted with RL-1 antibody (Nup214) or anti-p62 antibody. (B) The assay in C was repeated using GST-RanBP3 to precipitate Crm1. (C) NPC binding assays. Crm1-H<sub>6</sub> (40 nM) and/or GST-RanBP3 (400 nM) were overlayed onto digitonin-permeabilized BHK21 cells with either RanQ69L (2 &#x003bc;M), MVM-NS2 NES peptide (4 &#x003bc;M), or LMB (2 &#x003bc;M) at 24&#x000b0;C for 30 min. Crm1 and RanBP3 were identified by indirect immunofluorescence. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m. (D) Quantitation of Crm1 nuclear fluorescence shown in C. Data represent 50&#x02013;60 nuclei per sample &#x000b1; 1 SEM. (E) Crm1 binding to RanBP3 in the presence of Ran:GTP. Crm1 (500 nM) was incubated with GST-RanBP3, GST-F, or GST-R on glutathione&#x02013;Sepharose &#x000b1; RanQ69L (2 &#x003bc;M) and MVM NS2 NES peptide (4 &#x003bc;M), as shown, and bound or unbound proteins were immunoblotted.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>A model for RanBP3-assisted nuclear protein export. RanBP3 interacts with nuclear Crm1. The RBD of RanBP3 stabilizes the interaction of Crm1&#x02013;RanBP3 and Ran:GTP, increasing the affinity of the complex for NES-containing cargo. Ran:GTP binding to Crm1&#x02013;RanBP3 also releases inhibition to pore binding. The Crm1&#x02013;RanBP3&#x02013;NES&#x02013;Ran:GTP cargo complex translocates through the pore. On the cytoplasmic side of the pore, RanBP1 removes Ran:GTP allowing presentation to RanGAP, terminating export.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0101011.f9"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>